  "message": "Thanks for trying. Since your data is refueled and Mobile Data is ON, the most common remaining blockers for MMS are Wi‑Fi Calling interference, APN/MMSC configuration, or network mode. Let’s run three quick checks in this order. After each step, try sending an MMS and stop if it works.\n\n1) Turn OFF Wi‑Fi Calling (temporary test)\n- Check Wi‑Fi Calling status in Settings (or use check_wifi_calling_status). If it’s ON, turn it OFF (toggle_wifi_calling) and try sending an MMS.\n\n2) Verify APN/MMSC, reset if unsure\n- Check your APN settings (check_apn_settings) and confirm there is an MMSC field populated.\n- If MMSC is missing/blank or you’re unsure, reset APN to default (reset_apn_settings), then immediately reboot the phone (reboot_device). After the reboot, try sending an MMS.\n\n3) Ensure network mode isn’t limited to 2G/3G\n- Check your preferred network mode (check_network_mode_preference). If it’s set to 2G/3G, change it to 4g_5g_preferred (set_network_mode_preference) and try sending an MMS.\n\nIf it still fails after these, we’ll quickly confirm the messaging app has Storage and SMS permissions, and that Data Saver or a VPN isn’t interfering.\n\nPlease go through step 1 now and let me know: was Wi‑Fi Calling ON, did you turn it OFF, and what happened when you tried sending an MMS?",
  "steps": [
    "Check and temporarily turn OFF Wi‑Fi Calling; test MMS.",
    "Check APN for MMSC; if missing/incorrect, reset APN to default, then reboot; test MMS.",
    "Confirm network mode is 4g_5g_preferred; test MMS."
  ],
  "request": {
    "confirm_results": [
      "Wi‑Fi Calling status before/after and MMS result",
      "Whether MMSC was present; if APN was reset and phone rebooted; MMS result",
      "Network mode setting and MMS result (if steps 1–2 didn’t fix it)"
    ]
  }
}

