message.content:
{
  "message": "Thanks for the update. Your phone reporting “No SIM card detected” means the device isn’t detecting any active SIM/eSIM profile, so it cannot register to any network. Since we already enabled roaming and added data, we need to get the SIM recognized first. Once the phone sees the SIM, service, data, and MMS should return.",
  "why_this_matters": "If the SIM/eSIM isn’t detected, the phone cannot attach to a carrier abroad, so you’ll see no signal and data disabled.",
  "questions": [
    "Are you using a physical SIM card or an eSIM on this phone?"
  ],
  "steps_if_physical_sim": [
    {
      "title": "Power off",
      "do": "Completely power off the phone."
    },
    {
      "title": "Remove and inspect the SIM",
      "do": "Eject the SIM tray with the pin, remove the SIM, and check for dust or damage. Gently wipe the gold contacts with a soft, dry cloth."
    },
    {
      "title": "Reinsert carefully",
      "do": "Place the SIM flat in the tray (gold contacts facing down, notch aligned), reinsert the tray fully and firmly."
    },
    {
      "title": "Power on and wait",
      "do": "Turn the phone back on and wait up to 2 minutes. Verify: no airplane icon, signal bars appear, and a network type (4G/LTE/5G)."
    },
    {
      "title": "Confirm settings",
      "do": "Ensure Mobile Data = ON and Data Roaming = ON. Keep Wi‑Fi OFF for the test."
    },
    {
      "title": "Test",
      "do": "Try loading a website and sending an MMS with a small photo."
    }
  ],
  "steps_if_esim": [
    {
      "title": "Check eSIM line is present and ON",
      "do": "Go to Settings > Network & Internet (or Connections) > SIMs (or Mobile networks). Look for your carrier line and toggle it ON."
    },
    {
      "title": "If no eSIM profile is shown",
      "do": "Let me know. That means the eSIM profile may have been removed or is inactive and we’ll need to reprovision it."
    },
    {
      "title": "After enabling",
      "do": "Wait up to 2 minutes for signal bars and a network type to appear, then test browsing and MMS with Wi‑Fi OFF."
    }
  ],
  "please_report_back": [
    "Are you on a physical SIM or eSIM?",
    "After reseating (physical SIM) or enabling the eSIM line, do you still see “No SIM card detected” or do signal bars appear?",
    "Can you now load a website with Wi‑Fi OFF and send an MMS?"
  ],
  "next_if_still_no_sim_detected": "If your phone still shows “No SIM card detected” after reseating (for physical SIM) or if no eSIM profile is present/activates, I will transfer you to a human agent to assist with SIM/eSIM provisioning or replacement, since that requires steps I can’t perform here."
}

messages.content:
{
  "message": "Great—since you can browse on mobile data now but MMS still fails, your basic data/service is OK. The most common remaining causes are: (1) APN/MMSC configuration, (2) Wi‑Fi Calling interfering, or (3) the messaging app lacking required permissions. Let’s fix these in order.",
  "why_these_steps": "MMS requires a correct MMSC URL in your APN, and some phones/carriers don’t deliver MMS reliably when Wi‑Fi Calling is ON. The messaging app also needs Storage/Photos and SMS permissions to attach and send media.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Turn OFF Wi‑Fi Calling (temporarily)",
      "do": "Go to Settings > Wi‑Fi Calling and switch it OFF. Then, with Wi‑Fi OFF and Mobile Data ON, try sending an MMS again.",
      "reason": "Wi‑Fi Calling can sometimes block MMS while roaming."
    },
    {
      "title": "Check APN and MMSC; if unsure, Reset APN to default",
      "do": "Settings > Mobile Network (or Cellular) > Access Point Names (APN). Open your active APN and look for the MMSC field. If it’s blank/incorrect or you’re unsure, use the menu to ‘Reset to default’ APNs.",
      "important": "After resetting APNs, REBOOT your phone before testing MMS again.",
      "reason": "A missing/wrong MMSC URL prevents MMS from sending."
    },
    {
      "title": "Confirm messaging app permissions",
      "do": "Settings > Apps > Messaging (your default SMS/MMS app) > Permissions. Ensure Storage/Photos & media and SMS are allowed.",
      "reason": "Without these, the app can’t access media or send MMS."
    },
    {
      "title": "Retest MMS",
      "do": "Keep Wi‑Fi OFF, Mobile Data ON, Data Roaming ON, then send a small photo MMS.",
      "reason": "This verifies MMS over cellular data after the fixes."
    }
  ],
  "please_report_back": [
    "Is Wi‑Fi Calling now OFF?",
    "In the APN you saw, was the MMSC field present? If you reset APNs, did you reboot before testing?",
    "Are Storage/Photos & media and SMS permissions enabled for your messaging app?",
    "After these steps, does the MMS send successfully?"
  ],
  "next_if_still_failing": "If MMS still fails after resetting APNs and rebooting (with Wi‑Fi Calling OFF and permissions granted), tell me exactly what you see in the APN MMSC field and the error in the messaging app. If needed, I’ll escalate you to a human agent for deeper MMS/APN provisioning support."
}

